# THE line-ending rule. This repo is LF-only (tests/architecture.test.ts bans a raw
# CR in any src/ or server/ TypeScript file outright, and the biome formatter agrees),
# and every tracked text blob is stored LF. Without this line that only held for
# contributors who happened to set core.autocrlf=input: a default Windows clone
# (core.autocrlf=true) checked the whole tree out as CRLF, which reds that ban on
# thousands of untouched files and makes `biome check` report a format error on every
# file it is pointed at. An attribute is checked out with the repo, so it fixes the
# tree for everyone instead of asking each contributor to configure git correctly.
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binaries and leave them alone, so the PNGs and
# GLBs below are untouched; the *.bat exemption at the end of this file overrides it
# for the one blob that is deliberately CRLF (android/gradlew.bat).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated dense i18n resolved tables, plus the flat TranslationKey union emitted
# by the same build. These STAY committed (they are the tsc safety net + the runtime
# read source), but each is a machine-emitted artifact regenerated by
# `npm run i18n:build` / `npm run i18n:admin`. Marking them `linguist-generated`
# makes GitHub collapse them in pull-request diffs and excludes them from language
# statistics. Display hygiene only - not removal, not gitignore.
src/ui/i18n.resolved.generated/** linguist-generated
src/admin/i18n.resolved.generated/** linguist-generated
src/ui/i18n.catalog/translation_keys.generated.ts linguist-generated

# Windows batch scripts keep their CRLF endings: exempt from text conversion so an
# LF-normalized checkout (core.autocrlf=input) neither flags nor rewrites them.
*.bat -text

# Host diagnostic (electron/host_diag/): the shipped single-file script is pinned by
# the SHA-256 in dist/manifest.json, so git must never rewrite its bytes. Its sources
# are byte-faithful copies of the tool's upstream sandbox (mixed LF/CRLF) that the
# bundler normalizes at build time; exempt them too so a checkout cannot change them.
# The manifest is compared byte for byte by `host_diag_build.mjs --check` in the
# desktop build, so a Windows runner's core.autocrlf=true checkout (CRLF) failed the
# v0.44.0 Windows publish as "dist is stale"; it is exempt for the same reason.
electron/host_diag/dist/HostDiag.ps1 -text
electron/host_diag/dist/manifest.json -text
electron/host_diag/win/** -text
